{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1122315-25-story-hooks-named-after-80s-songs","id":1122315,"title":"25 story hooks named after 80s songs","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-07-29T06:26:49-07:00","id":1122315,"title":"25 story hooks named after 80s songs","content":"Inspired by Malkavian87 posting <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1121777-15-vampire-the-masquerade-stories-based-on-goth-songs\">15 Vampire: the Masquerade stories based on Goth songs</a>, it seems like a good time to dust off this list from 2009, originally posted on <a href=\"http://forum.rpg.net/showthread.php?t=478113\" target=\"_blank\">RPG.net</a> as part of the series that started the idea.<br /> <br /> Since the songs come from the 80s, some of these hooks were specifically created with <b>New Wave Requiem</b> in mind and use <b>Requiem</b> Clans and Covenants, but the majority should work with modern or historical settings, <b>Requiem</b> or <b>Masquerade</b>. Many of the problems facing vampires are as timeless as they are.<br /> <br /> <b>THE FRAMEWORK</b><br /> <br /> Hook: the problem, weird occurrence or opening situation which causes the coterie to get involved with events.<br /> Problem: the full nature of what's going on - what caused the hook to happen, and what forces are aligned against the coterie.<br /> Complications: Elements which are either part of the problem or external events which will make solving the problem not quite so straightforward as hitting the books or finding the killer.<br /> Sub-Plots: These are a few lines on the character drama that could be wrung out of the events described, so that each episode is more than just a mystery.<br /> Resolution: How to solve the mystery, kill the beast and finish the story. Usually some options are included to suit your players or allow for their creativity.<br /> Fun Stuff: This is just a list of some of the fun scenes or ideas the episode will let you play around with, to inspire your imagination.<br /> Theme: Vampire is always full of metaphor. This last section contains ideas on how the plot could be used as a reflection or life lesson for the coterie.<br /> <br /> Subplots recur through some, making it possible to create an entire Chronicle along these lines.<br /> <br /> The setting presumes a decent-sized city with a fairly typical mixture of vampire clans, covenants and other political blocs, and a coterie of characters who aren\u2019t at the head of the table. The list also assumes high-functioning Humanity for the coterie in general.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>THE LIST</b><br /> <br /> Staring At The Sea (The Cure)<br /> I Wanna Be Adored (The Stone Roses)<br /> Tainted Love (Soft Cell)<br /> Detonation Boulevard (Sisters of Mercy)<br /> Blue Monday (New Order)<br /> Original Sin (INXS)<br /> Cat Among the Pigeons (Bros)<br /> Johnny and Mary (Robert Palmer)<br /> Mad World (Tears for Fears)<br /> Boys From Brazil (Simple Minds)<br /> William, It Was Really Nothing (The Smiths)<br /> Synchronicity II (The Police)<br /> Vienna (Ultravox)<br /> Ghost Town (The Specials)<br /> She's Lost Control (Joy Division)<br /> Ashes to Ashes (David Bowie)<br /> Enjoy The Silence (Depeche Mode)<br /> Fade to Grey (Visage)<br /> To Cut a Long Story Short (Spandau Ballet)<br /> Town Called Malice (The Jam)<br /> Take On Me (A-Ha)<br /> Autobahn (Kraftwerk)<br /> SlowDive (Siouxsie and the Banshees)<br /> There Must Be an Angel (Eurythmics)<br /> 99 Luftballons (Nena)<br /> <br /> --<br /> <br /> <b>Staring At The Sea</b><br /> Hook: How do vampires cope? They know that they are monsters, unable to fully love or trust anyone, existing in perpetual darkness and hunting people for food. What keeps so many of them from contemplating suicide?<br /> Problem: One of the coterie, or an ally, suffers a painful loss. All remembered feelings of despair resurface, and it\u2019s all he can think about for nights on end. Is there really a worse Hell waiting than this?<br /> Complications: The crisis could easily be self-inflicted - a death caused by Frenzy, a reminder of the life he left behind - or it could have been orchestrated by a rival in Kindred politics, someone who knows his emotional weaknesses.<br /> Subplots: This vampire\u2019s death would create a gap in the city\u2019s web of power, potentially including a lapse in the control of information needed for the Masquerade. This gives other Kindred selfish reasons to want the vampire to carry on regardless of how he feels.<br /> Resolution: The crisis comes to a head and the rest of the coterie have to talk the vampire down.<br /> Fun Stuff: Imagery of martyrdom. Obviously suicide can be a touchy subject for some people, so consider how to present an idea like this.<br /> Theme: What gives life meaning?<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>I Wanna Be Adored</b><br /> Hook: A vampire with a major interest in the local music scene finds a young singer dying of an overdose, and she decides to Embrace him.<br /> Problem: The boy decides to exercise his newfound power, as fledgling Kindred often will. But as he was already fairly well known, this is likely to attract more attention than usual.<br /> Complications: He has no interest in a quiet unlife, and has read enough Anne Rice to consider playing with the vampire image to make himself famous.<br /> Subplots: The elders of the city want this situation dealt with quietly. And one of them is connected to the drug dealer whose product left the singer dying in the first place<br /> Resolution: Anyone who can bring him around without getting him killed will earn his sire\u2019s gratitude.<br /> Fun Stuff: Going behind the scenes of the low end of the music business, and the drugs trade for that matter. Hedonism beyond human levels of endurance.<br /> Theme: The drive to be noticed, to be remembered, can affect us all.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Tainted Love</b><br /> Hook: As one of the coterie feeds on a recurring vessel, the victim tells her that he loves her.<br /> Problem: He seems completely in earnest. He has never felt like this about anyone before, just seeing her makes his heart beat faster. She can tell he reacts to her physically. And he wants more than she can offer.<br /> Complications: Someone scheming against her would be delighted to use a potential breach of the Masquerade, and a possible emotional trainwreck for all involved, to damage her reputation.<br /> Subplots: Is she even capable of feeling love? And if so, would it be him? What drew her to feed from him more than once in the first place?<br /> Resolution: Cut all ties with him - if she can\u2019t make him forget her, she should find someone who can. Can she do that to him? Even if she does, his feelings are strong enough that he might remember if he ever sees her again.<br /> Fun Stuff: Conflicting emotions, romantic misunderstandings, a mortal pursuing a vampire. It could be played for pathos, comedy or pitch-black emotional horror.<br /> Theme: How much do you ever really know someone else, even someone you love, even someone who claims to love you?<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Detonation Boulevard</b><br /> Hook: Driving back from a club, the coterie see a car following them with its headlights off. As they react, it overtakes them, pulls ahead and races off.<br /> Problem: Chasing the lightless car, it turns in to a deserted side road and starts pushing ninety on the wrong side of the road, before hitting a wall and going into a tailspin. The driver stumbles out, bleeding from a cut across his broken nose, laughing.<br /> Complications: It seems he\u2019s not the only one, but part of a group thinking along the lines of J.G. Ballard\u2019s <b>Crash</b> - living and willing to die for the thrill of destruction.<br /> Subplots: And the coterie are not the first vampires to discover them. Another Kindred watches the devastation, spurring his fellows on to take greater risks for his own amusement.<br /> Resolution: By the traditions of the Kindred, the ringleader is doing nothing wrong. After all, only mortals are really being endangered, and most of them are endangering themselves willingly. So how do you justify stopping him?<br /> Fun Stuff: Car chases and the joys of meaningless danger and destruction.<br /> Theme: How far would you go for pleasure?<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Blue Monday</b><br /> Hook: Two of the city\u2019s younger vampires run an alternative night every Monday at a club. They forbid feeding from their crowd. They lack the strength to enforce the rules of Elysium, but the informal version stands because of their relative popularity, especially with unaligned Kindred.<br /> Problem: So there are several vampires in the club when someone finds a bloodless body in the men\u2019s room.<br /> Complications: Who the killer was doesn\u2019t matter right now. What matters is disposal - letting a body be discovered is considered more important in vampire law than actually killing someone, and none of the Kindred in the club want to spend a day in the cells if someone calls the police.<br /> Subplots: With almost a dozen vampires in the club, there are a lot of potential motives and other sources of conflict.<br /> Resolution: Find the killer. Was someone trying to ruin the club proprietors, or did they just come in thirsty?<br /> Fun Stuff: The practical issues of moving a body and covering up a murder, followed by a murder mystery where the characters themselves had a hand in wiping out all the forensic clues.<br /> Theme: Life, and death, don\u2019t stop to let you have a night off.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Original Sin</b><br /> Hook: A team of archaeologists based in the city made an interesting discovery during a dig in northern Turkey - an empty tomb where the main grave goods were ceramic jars containing dried blood. Dating places it around the time of early Greek expansion.<br /> Problem: Any vampire reading the description of the tomb is immediately reminded of a long-term haven, and this is from centuries before the founding of the Camarilla, the generally accepted time of earliest vampire civilisation. So who was this vampire?<br /> Complications: Seeing the retrieved pieces themselves makes any Kindred want to keep away from them and hold them at the same time. The desire to taste the dust of millennia-dried blood is as overpowering as sweet fresh vitae to a starving sleeper.<br /> Subplots: News of the find attracts the attention of numerous vampires and other beings with an interest in occult history - some claiming to be old enough to know some of the truth. Someone is bound to try and steal it, to sell it to the highest bidder, or to use it for some ritual purpose.<br /> Resolution: Negotiate peace between the factions and let the archaeologists work, or let the fighting intensify until someone steals or destroys the pieces.<br /> Fun Stuff: Fusty academics, unnerving occultists, and the mystery of the First Vampire.<br /> Theme: Where do we come from?<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Cat Among the Pigeons</b><br /> Hook: \u201cVampires In The City?\u201d is not the sort of headline you want to read in the evening newspaper.<br /> Problem: Assuming it wasn\u2019t actually one of the coterie making the mistake, someone - probably someone young and untested, but not necessarily - has left a bloodless body to be discovered in the coterie\u2019s hunting ground.<br /> Complications: The reporter involved has a decent, though not spotless, track record, and didn\u2019t want the overly sensational headline because, after all, who really believes in vampires? So he could be talked around, but the editor above him just wants to sell papers. Stronger pressure than most of the characters can exert would be needed to get him to back down.<br /> Subplots: Who really believes in vampires? A number of people who read the report with interest, and so now there are hunters sniffing around the coterie\u2019s backyard.<br /> Resolution: Find the killer, or someone else to shift the blame onto. Keep your heads down. Keep an eye on that paper...<br /> Fun Stuff: Tiptoeing around a frayed patch of the Masquerade\u2019s coverage, where a misstep could reveal far too much.<br /> Theme: When you live with secrets all the time, you can never fully relax, never tell the whole truth.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Johnny and Mary</b><br /> Hook: Two children sleeping in the park, both a little under ten, who look healthier and cleaner than runaways usually do. They explain that they ran away just before sunset last night. Because Dracula lives next door.<br /> Problem: Johnny and Mary are unshakeable in their belief that their new neighbour, Mr. Cole, is a vampire. He arrived at night in a big black car. Their dog, Scraps, went missing the next day. And Mrs. Donovan the divorced lady across the street looks really pale and sick. Their parents don\u2019t believe them, of course.<br /> Complications: They\u2019re right. Cole is a vampire, using the suburbs as a base for a small Ordo Dracul cell investigating the cemetery the houses are built over.<br /> Subplots: Johnny and Mary\u2019s parents, the Hendersons, have already called the police in to look for them and have their pictures on the evening news tonight.<br /> Resolution: Confront Cole and \u201cdiscourage\u201d him from making his lair somewhere so obvious.<br /> Fun Stuff: Jittery suburban kids who are ready to go Monster Squad on a vampire who is just a little more careless than most.<br /> Theme: The young and the old have much to learn from each other. And potentially much to fear.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Mad World</b><br /> Hook: After a fight at a bar, one of the coterie is unexpectedly low on blood. Seeking an easy victim, he creeps into an alley - and is run over by a car.<br /> Problem: Escaping the driver\u2019s attempts to get him to hold still and wait for an ambulance, he stumbles away - but everywhere he turns, he is struck with horror and pain, like the whole world is trying to drag him to his knees and destroy him. The car was driving so fast to get away from a demonstration turned riot a few blocks over.<br /> Complications: Some of the rioters have Molotov cocktails, so there are sudden flashes of fire. And the riot police are moving through the streets, ready with batons and tear gas.<br /> Subplots: He is not the only vampire in the crowd. Carthian and Invictus supporters clash over the sale of a local industry to asset-strippers which the demonstration was against.<br /> Resolution: Get out - get away - run - hide!<br /> Fun Stuff: Riots, riot police, familiar streets turning into a warzone as the character just wants to get away.<br /> Theme: Civilisation can be as thin a veneer as the Masquerade, and as dangerous when stripped away.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Boys From Brazil</b><br /> Hook: The press report an upswing in violence between drug gangs. Any criminal contacts the coterie have in the area go to ground.<br /> Problem: A new and violent gang seeks to take over the city's cocaine trade, cutting out existing middlemen and going directly to South American suppliers using their own transportation. Said existing middlemen include a gang in the pocket of a Primogen.<br /> Complications: The new gang is secretive, mobile, a step ahead of police surveillance and any other mortal agency that the Kindred could influence their way. If they\u2019re to be stopped, it must be through supernatural means.<br /> Subplots: They\u2019re not the only people taking out rivals - Kindred and mortal criminals alike use the war as an excuse to seize territory and settle scores. Someone known to the coterie is bound to end up in the crosshairs.<br /> Resolution: Work with the Primogen and his allies to stop the new gang, or with the up-and-comers to bring down the established order, or play the factions against each other.<br /> Fun Stuff: A deadly and lawless subculture defined by the struggle to control a highly addictive substance. Sound like anyone we know?<br /> Theme: How long can any structure survive in a world defined by violence and greed?<br /> (I resisted the temptation to make this about clones of Hitler.)<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>William, It Was Really Nothing</b><br /> Hook: A Mekhet scholar asks the coterie to steal an out-of-print book from a university library. They succeed - it isn\u2019t at all difficult, despite his concerns.<br /> Problem: The next night a Nosferatu asks for a favour. Then a Gangrel, another Mekhet, a Daeva... Never anything dangerous or complicated, never more than a night\u2019s work. But it\u2019s every night...<br /> Complications: The Kindred doing the asking are initially members of the Mekhet\u2019s own circle of allies, then their contacts, then their rivals trying to keep the coterie busy, then their rivals\u2019 rivals...<br /> Subplots: Other Kindred given to solving others\u2019 problems and trading favours do not appreciate being crowded out of their niche. And a step back might suggest a pattern to at least some of the favours.<br /> Resolution: Ask the Mekhet, as politely as one can, why the coterie are being treated like welcome go-betweens by vampires who must have others they could ask. Does it all connect to the pattern? Are they collecting pieces of a puzzle that, when assembled, will affect them rather than those they helped?<br /> Fun Stuff: Sudden popularity as the coterie find themselves much in demand.<br /> Theme: No good deed goes unpunished.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Synchronicity</b><br /> Hook: A member of the coterie picks up his phone to call someone - and that person calls him. Looking for a piece of information, he picks up a book at random and it falls open at exactly the page he needs. Everything he considers seems to happen, just like that. This phenomenon does not extend to any of the other Kindred, who suffer the usual mixture of good and bad luck.<br /> Problem: As the lucky happenstance continues, the affected vampire attracts the attention of an augur from the Circle of the Crone, who believes that he is marked for some strange destiny to be revealed before the next waxing moon.<br /> Complications: The augur isn\u2019t the only vampire with a theory, and most of them want to use the character\u2019s state for their own ends. Kindred that he owes a favour may well want it repaid now - because a run of luck like this doesn\u2019t last forever.<br /> Subplots: This can just as easily happen with things the vampire wouldn\u2019t want to occur. If he wants to avoid the Sheriff, for example, he\u2019ll bump into her on the way to a meeting. And events that he\u2019s neutral about can line up in this way too.<br /> Resolution: Prove the augur wrong, or right, or attempt to prove her wrong and watch the fates conspire. Either way, whatever the vampire attempts on the night of the new moon will succeed, or fail, spectacularly.<br /> Fun Stuff: Weird superstitions about luck don\u2019t seem as unlikely when you can predict a coin toss every single time.<br /> Theme: So much of our lives is down to luck, timing and the whim of others. What we do with the hand we\u2019re dealt is how we can define ourselves.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Vienna</b><br /> Hook: Seeking answers for a mystery spanning centuries, the coterie have to visit an old city to consult with a particular member of the Ordo Dracul.<br /> Problem: Just getting there would be problem enough, without the press of Blood Potency from every vampire in the city urging the coterie to flee. But worse, the Dragon is missing. Her childe, who is herself over a century old, suspects that rival investigators have abducted her or worse.<br /> Complications: Most of the vampires in the city were undead before the coterie\u2019s parents were born, some were old when their home city was founded, and they are at best polite about dealing with \u201cchilder\u201d. So their concerns are met with condescension and disdain.<br /> Subplots: And one of the Dragon\u2019s fellow elders uses her dealing with \u201cinterloper whelps\u201d as an excuse to strike at her reputation in absentia and set his own lackeys after the coterie.<br /> Resolution: Find the old Dragon, caught up in another decades-old feud reignited by her working with the coterie, and learn what you came for. And perhaps encourage her to leave the old city... while looking at the place they return to and seeing how it could easily become just as insular and dusty in the years to come.<br /> Fun Stuff: Donnish snobbery and elitism. Which might look rather like high school cliques if you look past the florid language.<br /> Theme: Are the old ways necessarily the best, or the new? Is innovation an end in itself? Is tradition?<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Ghost Town</b><br /> Hook: After riots, evidence of vampire-style killers and other upheavals, the Mayor lays a curfew on the wildest parts of the city.<br /> Problem: Police patrols cars glide along deserted streets, every cop inside looking to crack down in person and make arrests. The homeless are bussed off the streets, the addicts moved along, and the streets are suddenly empty, with the Kindred having to go unnoticed as well.<br /> Complications: As vampires with feeding rights in the curfew areas feel the hunger tighten its grip, rival Kindred in nearby neighbourhoods find themselves fighting off poachers.<br /> Subplots: And someone is bound to take advantage of the competition to accuse or set up an enemy.<br /> Resolution: Can you ride out the drought, end the curfew, or find new ways to hunt off the streets?<br /> Fun Stuff: Empty streets, thirsty vampires, trigger-happy cops. An increasingly sharp divide between haves and have-nots.<br /> Theme: Do you know what you have before it\u2019s gone?<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>She\u2019s Lost Control</b><br /> Hook: Another quiet night in Elysium. Until a major Harpy throws someone across the room.<br /> Problem: As she stalks out, she turns to one of the coterie and smirks at her. Now the allies of the apparently violently unpopular vampire want to know why.<br /> Complications: The other Harpies are worried about, and by, their jaded colleague. The Keeper of Elysium wants to be seen to be doing something, but has no intention of actually moving against her just for this. And the offended party wants someone easier to target his ire against.<br /> Subplots: Talk of Frenzy and Taint and Draugr fills every corner of Elysium, but the Harpies actively suppress it and attempt to turn the blame towards the victim.<br /> Resolution: Find the Harpy and have her explain her actions - it was an experiment to see what would happen, an exercise of how much power she really had. Not the response that the victim would want, of course, to be told that he can now be bullied with impunity. So how will he try to revenge himself?<br /> Fun Stuff: Sudden violence with everyone trying to politely ignore it.<br /> Theme: Power corrupts. How much power would it take to tempt you to abuse it?<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Ashes To Ashes</b><br /> Hook: Gilbert hasn\u2019t been seen by any of the city\u2019s other Kindred for a week. And now Madeleine has disappeared as well.<br /> Problem: It\u2019s easy for a vampire to disappear - after all, they exist in secret and rarely let their Kindred know their havens - and everyone gets worried when it happens. An investigation suggests that Madeleine was dragged into sunlight to die, but none of her known rivals seem likely to have done this. So what secret motive would someone have to destroy her? Not that all of the enemies of the Kindred even need a motive...<br /> Complications: There\u2019s nothing like a Kindred murder to remind vampires how precarious their existence can be. So many enemies, so many ways to destroy one with little or no evidence. So naturally, all of the city\u2019s Damned are on the guard around each other, quick to suspect motives and accuse others of all manners of crimes.<br /> Subplots: And then an Unbound Gangrel claims to see Gilbert in the barrens of the city. Does he know what happened to Madeleine?<br /> Resolution: There are many possible resolutions, depending on who killed Madeleine and why, whether Gilbert was involved or not, whether there are any copycat killings, the involvement of hunters or other, stranger enemies of the Kindred in general. But it could just as easily be a straightforward murder incited by lust, greed or envy. And what do you do with a Kindred-killer when you catch him?<br /> Fun Stuff: Frightened, paranoid, angry vampires.<br /> Theme: Violence begets violence, murder begets murder. What does it take to break the cycle?<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Enjoy The Silence</b><br /> Hook: The coterie cross paths with an unknown vampire - and while they can sense the blood and hunger in him, they do not feel the fight-or-flight instinct at all. Neither, apparently, does he.<br /> Problem: There are ways to mask the territorial instinct, but even if this new vampire has learned one of these methods, why does it continue when the coterie meet other Kindred?<br /> Complications: If the coterie tell other Kindred, the Ordo Dracul will want to study their condition, and the other covenants most likely would as well. What does it mean? Can the effect be replicated? Will it last? What if it has already begun to spread among the Kindred of the city?<br /> Subplots: The coterie hear reports of other strange effects - vampire weaknesses fading, or worsening, almost like symptoms of a disease as it progresses. Is that all they are?<br /> Resolution: The unknown vampire keeps away from Kindred society despite his ability to walk in without angering anyone. Who is he? Where did he come from? The coterie have to find him and find out why he can do this.<br /> Fun Stuff: Vampire mysticism and science colliding, becoming the unknown that other Kindred fear and envy.<br /> Theme: We accept some things unwillingly - that\u2019s just the way life is, after all. But what do we have to accept and what could we leave behind?<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Fade to Grey</b><br /> Hook: One of the coterie wakes after a normal day\u2019s sleep and, just over an hour later, receives a call asking why she missed an appointment. Checking her schedule it\u2019s there, but she doesn\u2019t remember agreeing to it.<br /> Problem: The Fog of Eternity seems to have fallen on her overnight. Her short-term memories are unclear or even missing entirely.<br /> Complications: Besides her confusion, she has let down allies and contacts, leaving them wondering what the problem is.<br /> Subplots: And then she meets a mortal she has no memory of at all, who claims to know her intimately and seems to know some of her secrets. Is he responsible for her amnesia? Can she trust him? Can she afford not to? Can she trust anyone who claims to know her, in fact?<br /> Resolution: Some of the city\u2019s elders are experts in navigating through the Fog - perhaps they can help. Perhaps this is a curse, the effects of a Kindred power or mortal sorcery. Perhaps something else has been impersonating her, even possessing her. Whatever the case, she has to find someone who knows the truth.<br /> Fun Stuff: The vampire\u2019s mind playing tricks on her - the Fog of Eternity on a small scale, and a taste of what it must be like for an elder vampire rising from torpor.<br /> Theme: If we lose our memories, who are we? Are we still the same people without our histories?<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>To Cut a Long Story Short</b><br /> Hook: A vampire wakes in a room he does not recognise, with blood drying on his clothes and his lips, as someone outside hammers on the door.<br /> Problem: Did he Frenzy earlier? Was he drugged while feeding? Did someone control him? But none of that matters right now - he has to escape. It could be an angry landlord outside, or building security, or a heavily-armed police raid...<br /> Complications: And outside of the room, the vampire has no idea where he is. Where can he hide? Who can he trust to call for help? And whose blood is this?<br /> Subplots: After escaping, the Kindred needs to piece together the events that brought him here. And perhaps someone else is following him now as well. And this could also be combined with Fade To Grey, so the victim discovers that he has been missing for several nights now.<br /> Resolution: Escape, and worry about how this happened later.<br /> Fun Stuff: A vampire on the run, alone, away from his normal territory.<br /> Theme: What can you truly rely on in life?<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Town Called Malice</b><br /> Hook: Research forces the coterie onto the road, and bad weather forces them to stop in a small town. And the locals are wary, suspicious, more than they should be.<br /> Problem: The town police arrive and tell the coterie to be about their business. All of them are armed... and some of them have stakes. It seems that everyone in town knows a vampire when they see one.<br /> Complications: Leaving as quick as they can, the Kindred find themselves driving into the storm, which forces them off the road. The town is the only nearby shelter from the approaching sunrise. It almost seems like the storm wants them to turn back. Is there a vampire in town, or something stranger still?<br /> Subplots: The locals are nervous but not all that frightened - outnumbering the coterie dozens to one, after all. Some of the younger townsfolk say they\u2019ve never seen a real vampire before, and find the Kindred fascinating rather than terrifying.<br /> Resolution: Make peace with the neighbours, and find the being responsible for breaking the Masquerade and luring them in. Someone needs a talking to.<br /> Fun Stuff: A breach in the Masquerade with a population of several hundred people.<br /> Theme: Keeping something completely secret is difficult, increasingly close to impossible the more people are involved. Something can always slip out.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Take On Me</b><br /> Hook: At some point, the coterie are bound to upset an elder of the city. Perhaps the Prince or equivalent, maybe another elder with her own power base and followers. Someone who can send a Sheriff or similarly powerful agent to bring them in...<br /> Problem: And this particular enforcer knows the Kindred well, but is not one himself. He\u2019s something... else.<br /> Complications: How well do the coterie know what they share the shadows with?<br /> Subplots: Besides a different supernatural beast hunting for the coterie with all an elder vampire\u2019s resources, there will be more interested parties among the Kindred, and possibly the others, taking an interest in the group due to the elder\u2019s proclamation.<br /> Resolution: Find the agent\u2019s weakness and use it against him, force him off your trail. Or go to the elder with a deal to offer.<br /> Fun Stuff: Fighting a monster working with the approval of a vampire elder.<br /> Theme: There are more things in Hell and Earth than are dreamt of...<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Autobahn</b><br /> Hook: Few vampires pay much attention to housing and road building, except when they need somewhere to bury a body. But more than usual notice when a new city bypass cuts through the territory claimed by an elder who has not been seen for decades.<br /> Problem: The elder\u2019s haven could be anywhere in several acres of undeveloped land, but the chance of workmen disturbing it threatens the Masquerade as well as the relative stability of the neighbourhoods bordering the domain. Something must be done. But what?<br /> Complications: Even at night, a major building site may not be deserted and is protected by security measures ranging from fences to cameras to guards. A search would take a small group of vampires several nights, at risk of discovery. And other coteries might have similar ideas about finding the elder, for different reasons.<br /> Subplots: And whose idea was it to run the road straight through the elder\u2019s territory?<br /> Resolution: Find the sleeping elder and... well, that\u2019s up to whoever finds him.<br /> Fun Stuff: Conducting a search unnoticed, clashing with rival coteries, and possibly meeting the elder himself.<br /> Theme: Even the oldest and strongest of our structures is not immune to decay or destruction.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>SlowDive</b><br /> Hook: The business section of the night\u2019s news reports a sudden downturn in the local economy.<br /> Problem: With the city\u2019s economy faltering, valued allies in business are suddenly helpless, invested resources crumble and Kindred with strong ties to the money market grow increasingly nervous.<br /> Complications: In the small community of the Damned, what affects a few prominent Kindred can reach out to affect many more. Even a vampire with no money or possessions of his own could be threatened as an elder sees his fortune tumble and seeks more permanent assets like a poor vampire\u2019s hunting grounds.<br /> Subplots: Is someone working the system to make the problem worse, manipulating businessmen to make bad decisions? Why?<br /> Resolution: Find anyone directly responsible and stop them, encourage spending, and make a killing - possibly literally - in the market as you manipulate businesses back into the black.<br /> Fun Stuff: Market failures, businesses crashing down, economic manipulations and a lot of angry vampires in suits.<br /> Theme: The love of money may not be the root of all evil, but it manages its share.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>There Must Be an Angel</b><br /> Hook: An Unbound gang, notorious for seeking easy prey near town and leaving no survivors, disappears. And no-one really mourns their passing... Until one of the gang stumbles into Elysium, cut and bloodied and wild-eyed with fear, gasping out a story about an avenging angel destroying the others.<br /> Problem: His story seems to be true. He really does remember escaping from a manlike figure that leapt down from a nearby roof to get between the gang and their prey, threw the leader across the street with superhuman strength and speed and drew a sword just before the survivor ran. Who is he? What is he?<br /> Complications: The Kindred are likely to be split on the meaning of the \u201cangel\u201d appearing, some wanting to research this further and others wanting to leave well alone. Some will pray for guidance... and what if they receive it?<br /> Subplots: Other \u201cangel\u201d sightings are rumoured throughout the city, of a mysterious stranger saving people from accidents and more mundane threats.<br /> Resolution: A daring group could confront the angel, seeking to learn the truth. And what will they discover when they do?<br /> Fun stuff: A mystery hunting the Kindred as they hunt mortals in secret. Fear of the unknown, religious uncertainty, doubt, faith and elemental curiosity. And possibly an angel.<br /> Themes: How will you be judged?<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>99 Luftballons</b><br /> Hook: A clash between world leaders has Kindred and kine fearing the threat of war.<br /> Problem: A young upstart among the Damned decides that this is the time to strike against an elder. Why not, when the world might end tomorrow?<br /> Complications: The war between the Kindred echoes the danger we all face - the would-be usurpers imitate the bullish speeches of mortal leaders and the elders respond with similar venom.<br /> Subplots: Through all this, the mortal populace carries on, looking fitfully to the skies. Anti-war graffiti covers walls, vigils for peace are held in city hall. Some party like there\u2019s no tomorrow while others stay home with their loved ones, making hunting in the open more difficult.<br /> Resolution: The Kindred conflict is in some ways cathartic - a fight that the coterie can do something about even as the threat of war hangs over the world. Its resolution could reflect the growing fear all around, with Kindred fighting in secret at the edges of a peace rally, and the opposing forces asking themselves if they really want to risk mutually assured destruction.<br /> Fun stuff: Fear of dangers beyond anyone\u2019s control, vampire or mortal alike. The struggle for some measure of influence, from peace rallies to the Mayor sending in the troops to the Kindred fighting in the shadows of a world that could be dust tomorrow.<br /> Themes: No matter how powerful you might be, there are forces in the world beyond your influence.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Craig Oxbrow<br /> <b>The Trinity Continuum</b> freelancer","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/221-craig-oxbrow","name":"Craig Oxbrow","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar221_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"608"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-29T06:40:04-07:00","id":1122318,"title":"","content":"I dare you to make a new thread with Nine Inch Nails songs!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13653-narr666","name":"Narr666","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"57"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-29T18:09:03-07:00","id":1122555,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div 